From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Flax <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 19:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] usb: gadget: composite: trace every ep0 setup packet via
 dynamic debug

Diagnostic for the Redfish Host Interface inventory push.  The TP26 BIOS
enumerates our RNDIS gadget during POST and issues SET_CONFIGURATION -- the
gadget logs "high-speed config #1" -- but then never sends RNDIS_MSG_INIT and
abandons the host-interface push ~25s later.  usb0 sees zero ethernet frames in
either direction.  The same device is bound and fully initialised by Linux
40 seconds later, so the descriptors themselves are acceptable to a normal
RNDIS host.

To find what the BIOS asks for between SET_CONFIGURATION and giving up we need
the ep0 conversation.  Upstream composite_setup() logs only the *non-core*
requests, and only under VDBG, which is gated on the VERBOSE_DEBUG compile-time
switch -- unusable on a production kernel.

Log every setup packet with DBG() (dev_dbg), which costs nothing unless enabled
at runtime, and promote the existing non-core VDBG to DBG for the same reason.

Enable at runtime with:
  echo 'file drivers/usb/gadget/composite.c +p' > \
      /sys/kernel/debug/dynamic_debug/control

Signed-off-by: Flax <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
---
--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/composite.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/composite.c
@@ -1788,6 +1788,16 @@
 	struct usb_function		*iter;
 	u8				endp;
 
+	/*
+	 * Trace every ep0 setup packet.  Upstream only logs the non-core
+	 * requests, and only under VDBG (VERBOSE_DEBUG), which is a compile-time
+	 * switch -- so there is no way to see what a host actually asks for on a
+	 * production kernel.  dev_dbg() keeps this free unless it is turned on at
+	 * runtime via dynamic_debug.
+	 */
+	DBG(cdev, "ep0-setup req%02x.%02x v%04x i%04x l%d\n",
+	    ctrl->bRequestType, ctrl->bRequest, w_value, w_index, w_length);
+
 	if (w_length > USB_COMP_EP0_BUFSIZ) {
 		if (ctrl->bRequestType & USB_DIR_IN) {
 			/* Cast away the const, we are going to overwrite on purpose. */
@@ -2200,7 +2210,7 @@
 			goto check_value;
 		}
 
-		VDBG(cdev,
+		DBG(cdev,
 			"non-core control req%02x.%02x v%04x i%04x l%d\n",
 			ctrl->bRequestType, ctrl->bRequest,
 			w_value, w_index, w_length);
